WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520
People have a general idea
of insanity or craziness or madness

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.520
that they associate with the idea of
schizophrenia.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.360
It's kind of an idea of wildness,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.600
not so much what
psychosis might actually be

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:20.080
but more of an idea of
violence or just being crazy.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.160
My name is Esme Weijun Wang,
I attended Yale University

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.000
and Stanford University
and I have been diagnosed with

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.240
schizoaffective disorder
bipolar type since 2013.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.320
Before I was diagnosed with
schizoaffective disorder

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.200
I had already been in
the mental health care system

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.800
for quite a number of years.
I'd been diagnosed with depression

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.000
and anxiety since I was about 15 or
16, and then I was diagnosed

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.440
with bipolar disorder
in my late teens.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.320
When I was finally diagnosed
with schizoaffective disorder

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.120
I felt a great sense of relief.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.200
It's quite common for people to
go through a number of diagnoses

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.600
before they reach
their final diagnosis.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.720
And I think that part of the
reason this happens is that

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.600
schizoaffective disorder
in particular, but also the

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:16.080
schizophrenias in general,
is a rather stigmatised diagnosis.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.480
And so often doctors
will resist that diagnosis

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.560
for as long as possible.
So they start with the

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.720
quote unquote, "easier diagnoses".
They might start with depression

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.920
and then move on to bipolar disorder
as they did in my case.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.080
People tend to think of schizophrenia
in a way that they don’t think about

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.960
other mental health diagnoses.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.160
When people think about depression,
they often think about the person

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.520
that has depression
as still themselves.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.160
When they think about schizophrenia
they tend to think about this person

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.360
who is who they were, but then kind
of scooped out and removed

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.120
and replaced by this awful disease
and the person who used to be there

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.680
and was a good and
normal person is gone.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:05.360
They're possessed as though by
a demon, as though in the movie

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:06.720
The Exorcist.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.720
Except instead of a demon,
they're replaced by schizophrenia.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:16.360
Because people think that there
is a kind of possession involved

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.800
in schizophrenia
or the schizophrenias,

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.720
they also believe that the person
has no autonomy over themselves

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.320
and no right to make decisions
over their own care.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.320
Therefore, forcible treatment
or forced hospitalisation

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.280
becomes necessary.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.640
I think that in order for
schizophrenia and the schizophrenias

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.360
to become less taboo in culture
there need to be more stories.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.760
I think that people need to come
out about their diagnoses,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.560
there need to be more narratives
about what the diagnosis means.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.080
But there also needs to be better
treatment and if there is better

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.600
treatment, if there are more stories,
if there are more narratives,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.920
If people can know that there
are people all around them

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.520
who have this diagnosis,
I think stigma will decrease.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.560
I use my accomplishments,
I use the way I dress,

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.640
I use my relationships
all as ways to prove,

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.960
"Look I'm still a worthy member
of society even though I have

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.200
this severe form of mental illness."

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:28.160
This also means that I'm part of the
problem when it comes to looking at

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.520
people who are perhaps less
quote unquote, "high-functioning".

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.640
The person on the bus who's
screaming, the houseless person

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.000
who has lost their job because there
is something very wrong with them

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.080
in terms of their mental health.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.920
And I know that, that is me

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.040
dealing with the stigma as well,
the self-stigma.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.120
That is their diagnosis,
as well as mine.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.000
I would like people to understand
that it's possible for you to know

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:05.440
somebody who has schizophrenia
or some form of the schizophrenias

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:06.640
without you knowing.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:12.760
People with that diagnosis, like me,
are everywhere and we are living

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.840
lives that are full and wonderful

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.800
and we also like blueberry pie.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.780
